Viewing and Comparing Changes
1. git diff: Show file differences not yet staged.

2. git status: Show the state of your working directory.

3. git show <commit_id>: Show details about a specific commit.

Staging and Committing Changes
4. git add <file_path>: Add file(s) to the staging area.

5. git commit -a -m "commit_message": Commit all tracked changes with a message.

6. git commit --amend: Modify the last commit.

Branching and Switching
7. git branch: List branches.

8. git branch -D <branch_name>: Forcefully delete a branch.

9. git checkout -b <branch_name>: Create and switch to a new branch.

10. git checkout <branch_name>: Switch to an existing branch.

11. git checkout <commit>: Switch to a specific commit.

Merging and Rebasing
12. git merge: Merge branches together.

13. git rebase -i: Rebase interactively, rewrite commit history.

14. git rebase <branch_name>: Rebase the current branch onto another branch.

Remote Operations
15. git push origin <branch_name>: Push a branch to a remote.

16. git pull: Fetch and merge remote changes.

17. git fetch: Fetch changes from the remote repository without merging.

Undoing Changes
18. git reset HEAD~1: Undo the last commit, preserving changes locally.

19. git reset --soft HEAD^: Undo the last commit, but keep the changes.

20. git reset --hard: Reset everything to a previous commit, erasing all uncommitted changes.

21. git revert <commit_id>: Create a new commit that undoes the changes of a specific commit.

Stashing
22. git stash: Stash changes for later.23. git stash pop: Apply and remove stashed changes.

Cherry-Picking
24. git cherry-pick <commit_id>: Apply changes from a specific commit.

Branch Tracking
25. git branch --set-upstream-to <remote_branch>: Set the upstream branch to the specified remote branch.

